# installer-base.pkgs — always-on core toolset for the INSTALLED system + installer env
#
# Target: Debian Testing (Forky) arm64 / cixmini MS-R1 + Orion O6/O6N.
# Scope:  packages that must be present on every installed NCZ-OS box AND in the
#         installer runtime (esp. the new r130 choose-disk flow needs reliable
#         lsblk/blkid/parted/nvme-cli to enumerate and label targets).
#
# This is a strict SUBSET of rescue.pkgs (the rescue partition is the superset).
# Format: one binary package per line; blank lines and '# ...' comments ignored.
# Consume with:  grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' manifests/installer-base.pkgs
#
# NOTE: remote-access (openssh-server, inetutils-telnetd/openbsd-inetd, busybox)
#       is already provisioned by post-install/36-telemetry.sh + 35-ssh.sh per
#       CLAUDE.md directive 9; listed here only to document the contract.

# --- initramfs builder ---------------------------------------------------
# dracut is the PRIMARY initramfs builder as of 26.7 (metal-validated on O6N,
# 7.2.0-rc7-sky1-ncz, 2026-08-16). It is seeded HERE, explicitly, because the
# kernel package only expresses a PREFERENCE:
#     Depends: dracut | initramfs-tools | linux-initramfs-tool
# An alternation is satisfied by whichever alternative the offline mirror
# actually contains. Before this line the mirror carried initramfs-tools and
# only dracut-install (a helper, not the builder), so an install would have
# silently fallen back to initramfs-tools and nobody would have seen a warning.
# Seeding it makes the mirror match the intent.
#
# initramfs-tools is deliberately NOT listed: it is no longer the builder, and
# it survives where it is still useful — the RESCUE partition toolkit
# (manifests/rescue.pkgs) for repairing a target system's initrd.
dracut
dracut-core

# --- DKMS accelerator rebuilds ----------------------------------------------
# CIX GPU/NPU accelerator drivers are registered and rebuilt by the base
# post-install hook path (79-dkms-prep, 82-mali-gpu, 83-panthor-gpu,
# 86-cix-dkms-register). Only cix-npu-driver-dkms is a real .deb with
# Depends: dkms; Mali, Panthor, and VPU are source-staged directly by hooks.
#
# DKMS is not enough by itself. 79-dkms-prep rebuilds the shipped kernel
# header tree's Yocto host tools, and the accelerator DKMS packages then build
# real modules during install. Those steps need a native compiler/toolchain;
# Mali's upstream gpu.mk also invokes aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc explicitly. Seed
# the tools here so the post-install path works offline and does not leave
# cix-npu-driver-dkms half-configured for hook 25's purge sweep.
#
# Keep this in base rather than desktop so console/non-desktop installs and
# future kernel upgrades have the same accelerator rebuild path.
dkms
build-essential
gcc
gcc-aarch64-linux-gnu
make
flex
bison
bc
libelf-dev
libselinux-dev

# --- disk / partition identify (required by choose-disk UI) ---
util-linux          # lsblk, blkid, wipefs, fdisk, sfdisk, cfdisk, lscpu
parted
gdisk
nvme-cli
smartmontools

# --- hardware identify ---
pciutils            # lspci
usbutils            # lsusb
dmidecode
lshw

# --- filesystems + recovery ---
btrfs-progs         # installed root is btrfs — must be able to fsck/mount its own root
e2fsprogs
testdisk            # testdisk + photorec

# --- boot management ---
efibootmgr

# --- network / diag ---
dbus                 # compatibility package; dbus-broker owns the system bus when enabled
dbus-broker          # system bus owner for base/console and desktop; 91-dbus-broker enables it
iwd                  # sinty-nm WiFi backend
wireguard-tools      # sinty-nm VPN backend
network-manager      # fallback network owner when assets/sinty-nm/sinty-nmd is absent
netplan.io           # 33-network.sh writes netplan NetworkManager renderer config for fallback
iproute2
net-tools
iputils-ping
ethtool
tcpdump
mtr-tiny
curl
wget
rsync
firmware-realtek
firmware-linux-free
firmware-misc-nonfree

# Generic NIC firmware for real Sky1 boards.  The ISO also stages rtl_nic blobs
# as raw assets for 12-sky1-firmware.sh, but the base/console rootfs must be
# self-contained when desktop.squashfs is deselected.

# --- operator quality-of-life ---
htop
tmux
vim
mc
tree
jq
lsof

# --- remote access (provisioned by post-install; documented here) ---
openssh-server
inetutils-telnetd
openbsd-inetd
# Static busybox for the UNWEDGEABLE failsafe console (post-install/37-failsafe-access.sh):
# extracted (not installed) into /opt/ncz-failsafe so a /usr/lib clobber that wedges
# every dynamic daemon still leaves a static root recovery shell on telnet :2323.
busybox-static

# zstd: post-install/72-rescue-partition.sh unpacks rescue-rootfs.tar.zst on the
# INSTALLED system and hard-requires the binary. Ubuntu's base shipped it, Debian
# forky's does not, so without it 72 aborts, no RESCUE_READY marker is written,
# ncz-refind-refresh then refuses to write /EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI, and late.sh
# correctly declares the install unbootable -- after the base system is on disk.
zstd

# --- NCZ kernel channels ---
# post-install/10-our-kernel.sh installs the kernel with apt. Until now the
# only source for these was the Buildkite repo over the NETWORK, which meant
# (a) a fresh install silently got whatever version Buildkite happened to be
# serving rather than the kernel staged on the ISO, and (b) the squashfs base
# layer could not build at all without network -- the LTS->legacy rename made
# that visible immediately as "E: Unable to locate package
# linux-image-cixmini-legacy", because the renamed package did not exist
# upstream yet.
# Seeding them here pulls the locally built .debs (published into the vendor
# mirror from build/kernel-debs by build/build-vendor-mirror.sh) into the
# offline closure, so the ISO ships the kernel it was built with.
linux-image-cixmini
cixmini-boot

# --- locales ---------------------------------------------------------------
# WITHOUT THIS PACKAGE THE SHIPPED SYSTEM HAS NO USABLE LOCALE.
#
# Measured on the built layers 2026-08-18: the only compiled locale present was
# /usr/lib/locale/C.utf8; there was no /usr/share/i18n, no locale-gen, no
# locale-archive, and /etc/default/locale was zero bytes. Meanwhile
# post-install/23-locale-env.sh sets LANG=LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 -- a locale that
# did not exist, so setlocale() failed and everything fell back to C. The hook
# whose entire purpose is to stop the "not a UTF-8 locale" fallback was
# pointing at a locale nobody had generated.
#
# In the base manifest rather than desktop.pkgs because the server variant
# needs a working locale just as much as the desktop does.
locales

# --- console font (HiDPI text console) -----------------------------------
# console-setup SHIPS THE TERMINUS CONSOLE FONTS that
# post-install/95-console-font-autosize.sh selects (Lat15-Terminus*/
# Lat15-TerminusBold32x16 on a 2160p panel). Seeded explicitly rather than
# left to the hook's own apt-get: on a console-only install the text console
# IS the user interface, and if the mirror lacks these the hook silently
# leaves 8x16 "Fixed" on a 4K display (measured on .66, 2026-08-22 --
# unreadable). kbd provides setfont itself.
console-setup
kbd
